From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fardjad Davari <public@fardjad.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 05:12:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix: nudge repeated deterministic tool input failures

X-Jcode-Patch-Intent: Nudge repeated deterministic tool failures without stopping turns
X-Jcode-Patch-Kind: personal-fix
X-Jcode-Patch-Depends-On: 0002-candidate-add-hook-context-and-post-tool-transform.patch
X-Jcode-Patch-File: 1010-personal-fix-stop-repeated-deterministic-tool-input-failures.patch

Patch intent:
After three matching typed deterministic failures, return feedback in the tool
result rather than ending the turn. Before reexecution, suppress only effective
inputs observed failing as part of a repeated signature. Corrected inputs and
alternative tools remain executable. Keep a 32-dispatch window and reset it for
each turn. Match categorized panel/applet failures by category and uncategorized
failures by exact effective input and error. Serialize calls by effective tool
and input; nested batches share guard evidence without serializing different
siblings. Cancellation releases the permit. Unknown tools use exact matching.
Neither turn loop has a repeat-guard-specific abort.

Why it exists:
Repeated invalid inputs can otherwise consume provider requests without
progress. The third matching typed failure gives the model actionable tool
feedback; later attempts of an observed failing input receive tool feedback
without reexecution, while corrected arguments and other tools can continue.
Classification remains explicit through typed errors, not inferred from error
text. Panel and applet deterministic input failures are categorized; unknown
tool names are typed but uncategorized. Runtime and other untyped tool errors
remain unclassified. This adds no global provider-loop cap.

Upstream integration points:
The change extends tool::retry_guard and wraps Registry::execute dispatches,
including nested BatchTool calls. Per-effective-input async permits serialize
matching dispatches, release on cancellation, and retain concurrency for
independent batch siblings. Agent::run_turn and
Agent::run_turn_streaming_mpsc reset the shared per-turn guard and deliver
repeat feedback as tool results without a guard-specific abort. PanelTool and
AppletTool expose UI schemas and validate their inputs; OpenAI strict-schema
normalization requires nullable panel fields while dynamic applet node fields
remain non-strict. Panel and applet deterministic input errors use stable
categories; Registry types unknown tool names for exact matching. Runtime
failures remain untyped. The guard depends on hook context established by
patch 0002.

Update guidance:
Keep failure classification limited to explicit typed input errors and unknown
tool names. Preserve exact effective-input-and-error matching for uncategorized
failures and stable categories for panel/applet input failures. Suppress only
an effective input observed failing in a repeated signature; corrected and
unseen inputs must remain executable. Keep tool/category separation, the
32-dispatch window, per-turn reset, cloned-registry guard state, nested batch
dispatch sharing, and cancellation-safe per-input serialization. Preserve
concurrency for distinct inputs and release permits when calls are canceled.
Keep both turn loops free of repeat-guard-specific aborts. Do not add a global
provider-loop cap or classify runtime failures. Keep the UI tool schemas aligned
with their deserializers, nullable panel fields accepted, and dynamic applet
fields permissive. The focused guard tests include
three_matching_typed_failures_nudge_and_block_only_observed_input and
categorized_failures_ignore_arguments_and_error_text_but_not_category. The batch
suite includes batch_retry_guard_suppresses_repeated_subcall_and_keeps_siblings
for same-input serialization and independent sibling progress, and
retry_guard_releases_same_input_permit_after_cancelled_dispatch to verify
cancellation releases the permit for a matching input. The recovery tests
ui_errors_nudge_both_turn_loops_across_changed_inputs_and_suppress_only_repeats
and repeated_deterministic_input_errors_nudge_without_stopping_both_turn_loops
cover both loop implementations, corrected inputs, and suppression feedback.

